  "name": "'s or of: who or what does it belong to?",
  "level": "B1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "Use 's for people and animals (my sister's car, the students' books) and of for things (the end of the film). Time words also take 's: today's news.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "'s",
    "the end of the film",
    "today's news"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "whose? / which?",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "people, animals: owner + 's",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "things: the thing + of + owner"
      },
      {
        "text": "plural with -s: s'"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "my sister<span class=\"vp-verb\">'s</span> car · the students<span class=\"vp-verb\">'</span> books · the end <span class=\"vp-prep\">of</span> the film · today<span class=\"vp-verb\">'s</span> news",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "For <strong>people and animals</strong>, put the owner first and add <strong>'s</strong>: <em>my sister's car, Tom's bike, the dog's bowl.</em> It answers the question <em>Whose?</em>",
        "tag": "people and animals: 's"
      },
      {
        "html": "A <strong>plural noun that ends in -s</strong> only gets an apostrophe: <em>the students' books</em> (many students). Compare: <em>the student's books</em> (one student). An <strong>irregular plural</strong> adds 's: <em>the children's toys, the women's team.</em>",
        "tag": "plural"
      },
      {
        "html": "For <strong>things</strong>, use <strong>of</strong>. The thing comes first: <em>the end of the film, the top of the hill, the door of the car, the name of the street.</em>",
        "tag": "things: of"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Time words</strong> also take 's: <em>today's news, yesterday's meeting, a week's holiday.</em> After a plural: <em>two weeks' holiday.</em>",
        "tag": "time words"
      },
      {
        "html": "<em>A friend of mine</em> means \"one of my friends\". After <em>of</em>, use <em>mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs</em> or a name with 's: <em>a friend of Tom's.</em> Not <em>a friend of me.</em>",
        "tag": "a friend of mine"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>the car of my sister</em> → ✓ <em>my sister's car</em> · ✗ <em>the students book</em> / <em>the students's books</em> → ✓ <em>the students' books</em> (many students) · ✗ <em>the childrens toys</em> / <em>the childrens' toys</em> → ✓ <em>the children's toys</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "With things, you can also put two nouns together: <em>the car door, the film ending, the street name.</em> The short form can stand alone when the meaning is clear: <em>Whose is this phone? — It's Tom's.</em> For a name that ends in <em>s</em>, both <em>James's flat</em> and <em>James' flat</em> are fine. For <em>my, your, his</em> before a noun, see <a href=\"/possessivartikel\">Possessive words</a>. Be careful: <em>'s</em> can also mean <em>is</em>: <em>Tom's here</em> (= Tom is here) or <em>Tom's flat</em> (= the flat of Tom).",
